@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
+/* Decision 0234 (mt3, stage B): the imports that the giant lock (runtime/fle_gil.h) is NOT taken for. One X(Name) per line; harness/gil/gen_wrap.py reads this file and makes a pass-through wrapper for each name, the others take the lock.
+ * An import is listed only when it reads and writes NO state of the runtime that another Worker can write: its arguments and results are registers of the calling Worker (R[], the thread-local block) and guest memory that the call names, the thread
+ * of the calling Worker, or a clock that is already safe to read from every Worker. Why this is not a shortcut: the lock was held 13% of the time and the Workers waited for it 30-60% of the time (hybrid, 80 s, 2 runs); 12 of the 16.7 million
+ * acquisitions of the first Worker were these calls (IntersectRect 9.5 million, GetTickCount 2.1 million, GetCurrentThreadId 0.37 million in 80 s). The table that says why each name qualifies is docs 0234q section 2; tests/gil_lockfree.rs checks that the
+ * body of each listed function calls nothing outside the list of calls that were read there (a new call in one of these functions fails the test: the review has to be done again).
+ * fle_user_nowin.c: pure functions of the rectangles in guest memory (fle_stop on the stop path only). */
+X(SetRect)
+X(IntersectRect)
+X(UnionRect)
+X(OffsetRect)
+X(InflateRect)
+X(EqualRect)
+X(PtInRect)
+X(SubtractRect)
+/* fle_sys.c: the clock. With the real clock (the only one when there is more than one Worker) a read is the page's clock (performance.timeOrigin + performance.now()) made monotonic across the Workers by an atomic maximum (fle_clock_real_now); a read does not move it. */
+X(GetTickCount)
+X(timeGetTime)
+X(QueryPerformanceCounter)
+X(QueryPerformanceFrequency)
+/* fle_imports.c: the id of the calling thread: the Worker's own `cur` (thread-local) and the number of threads (only ever grows). */
+X(GetCurrentThreadId)
